/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A triangulation system is a series of triangles joined side to side.
   - The common figures are a chain of single triangles (quick, but with no check beyond the sum of each triangle's angles), braced quadrilaterals (four stations with both diagonals observed, the strongest and most used for a long, narrow strip)
   - Typical values quoted in Indian textbooks.
   - L is the measured horizontal length at mean height H above mean sea level, R the earth's radius (about 6370 km), and L₀ the length at mean sea level.
   - In trilateration every side of every triangle is measured by EDM and reduced to the same surface.
   - Coordinates are carried through the net like a traverse.
   - β is the whole-circle bearing of AB measured clockwise from north.
*/
