/* book Worked example: Q: In a triangulation triangle ABC, AB is a base measured as 500.000 m (horizontal) at a mean height of 250 m above mean sea level. The observed angles, of equal weight, are A = 62°18′10″, B = 57°42′05″ and C = 60°00′00″. A has coordinates E 5000.000 m, N 3000.000 m and the bearing of AB is 75°30′00″; C lies to the left of AB looking from A. (a) Adjust the angles. (b) Reduce the base to mean sea level (R = 6370 km). (c) Find BC and AC. (d) Find the coordinates of B and C, and check C from B.
   1. (a) Sum of angles = 62°18′10″ + 57°42′05″ + 60°00′00″ = 180°00′15″. The triangle is small, so the spherical excess is negligible and the error is +15″: subtract 5″ from each. A = 62°18′05″, B = 57°42′00″, C = 59°59′55″ (sum 180°00′00″).
   2. (b) Correction = L H ÷ R = 500.000 × 250 ÷ 6 370 000 = 0.020 m. Base at mean sea level AB = 500.000 − 0.020 = 499.980 m.
   3. (c) sin A = 0.88540, sin B = 0.84526, sin C = 0.86601. BC = AB sin A ÷ sin C = 499.980 × 0.88540 ÷ 0.86601 = 511.17 m.
   4. AC = AB sin B ÷ sin C = 499.980 × 0.84526 ÷ 0.86601 = 488.00 m.
   5. (d) Bearing AB = 75°30′00″: sin = 0.96815, cos = 0.25038. EB = 5000.000 + 499.980 × 0.96815 = 5484.06 m; NB = 3000.000 + 499.980 × 0.25038 = 3125.18 m.
   6. C is to the left, so bearing AC = 75°30′00″ − 62°18′05″ = 13°11′55″: sin = 0.22833, cos = 0.97358. EC = 5000.000 + 488.00 × 0.22833 = 5111.43 m; NC = 3000.000 + 488.00 × 0.97358 = 3475.11 m.
   7. Check from B: bearing BA = 255°30′00″, and turning clockwise through B gives bearing BC = 255°30′00″ + 57°42′00″ = 313°12′00″: sin = −0.72897, cos = 0.68455.
   8. EC = 5484.06 + 511.17 × (−0.72897) = 5484.06 − 372.63 = 5111.43 m; NC = 3125.18 + 511.17 × 0.68455 = 3125.18 + 349.92 = 3475.10 m. The two routes agree within 0.01 m, the rounding of the sines.
   Answer: Adjusted angles 62°18′05″, 57°42′00″ and 59°59′55″; base at mean sea level 499.980 m; BC = 511.17 m and AC = 488.00 m; B (5484.06, 3125.18) and C (5111.43, 3475.11) m, checked from B within 0.01 m. */
