/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The rule of surveying is to work from the whole to the part: first a sparse frame of strong control over the whole area, then denser and weaker control inside it, and detail last.
   - Control is graded by order.
   - Each control point is a permanent mark (a bolt in concrete, a pillar, a mark on a culvert) with a description: a sketch and measured distances to two or three nearby features, so it can be found and used again.
   - A closed traverse either returns to its start (a loop) or ends on another known station, so its error can be found; an open traverse has no check and is not used for control.
   - l is a line's length and β its whole-circle bearing.
   - Vertical control is carried from benchmarks of the national levelling network by differential levelling in closed loops or between two benchmarks, with balanced sights.
   - Where levelling is impractical (hill tops, across a gorge) trigonometric heights from reciprocal vertical angles give lower-order vertical control.
*/
