/* book exam traps: Starting detail before the control is closed and adjusted. An error found later cannot be removed from the detail already fixed. | Using (n − 2) × 180° for the wrong set of angles. It is the sum of interior angles; exterior angles of a loop sum to (2n + 4) × 90°. | Distributing the closing error with the same sign as the error. The correction has the opposite sign, so the sums become zero. | Quoting the closing error without the length. 0.05 m is excellent on 935 m and poor on 50 m; the relative precision 1 in (Σl ÷ e) is what is compared with the specification. | Adjusting a traverse whose closing error is beyond the limit. It is remeasured, not adjusted; adjustment shares only small random errors. */
